    licences still match what is actually compiled in.

================================================================================
Engine provenance -- unresolved
================================================================================

NewBlackbox declares Apache-2.0 for its whole repository. Tracing that
declaration upstream does not support it:

  * asLody/VirtualApp, credited by NewBlackbox as the original framework, has
    no LICENSE file. Its README requires commercial authorisation to be
    purchased, states the technology belongs to a named company, and threatens
    prosecution for unauthorised commercial or app-store use. It is
    proprietary, not open source.

  * FBlackBox/BlackBox, the origin of the top.niunaijun.blackbox namespace used
    here, also has no LICENSE file; the GitHub API reports license: null. The
    original niunaijun/BlackBox repository now returns 404.

  * Bcore's Java sources carry no copyright headers at all.

On this evidence NewBlackbox was very likely not in a position to grant
Apache-2.0, so licenses/NewBlackbox-Apache-2.0.txt may not confer the rights it
appears to.

This is a statement of what was found in the source, not legal advice. It must
be reviewed by a lawyer before any distribution. The VirtualizationEngine
adapter boundary exists precisely so the backend can be replaced if this cannot
be cleared.
